    "consumed_by_exp_id": "64d19f94",
    "description": "# Future Directions \u2014 Rips Filtration \u2194 Tropical Valuation Bridge\n\nThese conjectures extend `Catalog/Bridges/RipsTropicalProfile.lean`, which establishes that\nthe Vietoris\u2013Rips metric filtration is recorded by a **monotone edge-count profile** equal to\nthe cardinality of a sublevel set of the **max-plus tropical birth valuation** `tropBirth`,\nwith path concatenation realized as tropical addition (`max`) and connectivity satisfying the\ntropical strong-triangle inequality (`ripsReachable_trans_max`).\n\nEach item below is stated to be falsifiable and formalizable in Lean 4 with Mathlib.\n\n## C1 \u2014 The single-linkage connectivity birth is a genuine ultrametric\nDefine, for a finite metric space, the **connectivity birth**\n`connBirth x y := sInf { \u03b5 : \u211d | (ripsGraph \u03b1 \u03b5).Reachable x y }` (with the convention\n`connBirth x x = 0`). Conjecture: `connBirth` is an ultrametric, i.e.\n`connBirth x z \u2264 max (connBirth x y) (connBirth y z)`, and it is symmetric with\n`connBirth x y = 0 \u2194 x = y`. The strong-triangle inequality should follow from\n`ripsReachable_trans_max` together with `csInf` monotonicity. This upgrades the\nfiltration-level tropical triangle inequality already proven to a bona-fide ultrametric\nobject, directly instantiating the `UltraNormObj`-style data of\n`CategoricalTropicalUltrametric.lean`.\n\n## C2 \u2014 The edge-count profile is a step function with jumps exactly at pairwise distances\nConjecture: for a finite set `S`, `ripsProfile S` is right-continuous, locally constant off\nthe finite set `D = { dist x y | x,y \u2208 S, x \u2260 y }`, and `ripsProfile S` strictly increases at\n`\u03b5 = d` for each `d \u2208 D` (counting multiplicity = number of ordered pairs at that exact\ndistance). Equivalently, `ripsProfile S \u03b5 = \u2211_{d \u2208 D, d \u2264 \u03b5} mult(d)`. This characterizes the\nprofile as the cumulative distribution function of the multiset of pairwise distances \u2014 the\n\"tropical spectrum\" of the point cloud.\n\n## C3 \u2014 Profiles are a complete invariant up to the distance multiset, but NOT up to isometry\nConjecture (two parts): (a) two finite point clouds have identical edge-count profiles for all\n`\u03b5` **iff** they have the same multiset of pairwise distances; (b) there exist non-isometric\nfinite metric spaces with identical profiles (homometric sets / the \"turnpike\" ambiguity).\nPart (a) is a clean `iff` reachable by current methods; part (b) is a concrete finite\ncounterexample (e.g. classic homometric sets on \u2124), provable by `decide`/`norm_num` once the\nprofile is made computable over \u211a.\n\n## C4 \u2014 Tropical functoriality of profiles under 1-Lipschitz maps\nConjecture: if `f : \u03b1 \u2192 \u03b2` is 1-Lipschitz and injective on `S`, then\n`ripsProfile (S.image f) \u03b5 \u2265 ripsProfile S \u03b5` for all `\u03b5` (contractions can only create or\npreserve edges, never destroy them). More strongly, profiles define a functor from the\ncategory of finite metric spaces with 1-Lipschitz maps to monotone `\u2115`-valued profiles,\nmirroring the `TropHom` monotonicity axiom of `CategoricalTropicalUltrametric.lean`. The\nexpected proof injects the `S`-edge filter into the `f(S)`-edge filter via `dist_image_le`.\n\n## C5 \u2014 Tropical stability: profiles are 1-Lipschitz in the Hausdorff/perturbation scale\nConjecture: if every point of `S` is moved by at most `\u03b4` (a `\u03b4`-perturbation `S'`, paired by a\nbijection), then the profiles interleave: `ripsProfile S (\u03b5 - 2\u03b4) \u2264 ripsProfile S' \u03b5 \u2264\nripsProfile S (\u03b5 + 2\u03b4)`. This is the edge-count shadow of the persistence stability theorem and\nconnects to `sphere_perturbation_stability` in `MetricFiltration.lean`. In tropical terms, a\n`\u03b4`-perturbation shifts every birth valuation `tropBirth` by at most `2\u03b4` in the `WithBot \u211d`\nmetric, so sublevel-set cardinalities shift monotonically \u2014 a quantitative, fully finitary\nrobustness statement.\n",
